course: dictionary
lesson: 1841
title: The snail-shaped ear
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: The cochlea is a part of your inner ear. It is a small tube that spirals round like a snail shell, and it is filled with liquid. The name is a clue: look for the spiral shape when you meet this word.
explain: «Sound is vibration in the air: it shakes the eardrum, three tiny bones called the hammer, anvil and stirrup pass it on, and hair cells in the snail-shaped cochlea turn it into nerve signals.» Read the words around cochlea and ask what job it does. It turns sound into nerve signals, and those signals carry the message to the brain so we can hear.
explain: «Very loud sounds can damage the hair cells in the cochlea, which in people do not grow back; ringing ears after fireworks are a warning sign, and ear defenders help.» The clues are damage, warning sign and defenders. So the cochlea is delicate, and protecting your ears from loud noise matters.
fact: The cochlea is in the inner ear, after the outer ear and the middle ear.
fact: Inside the cochlea, sound vibrations are turned into nerve signals.
fact: Nerve signals travel from the cochlea to the brain, so we can hear.
fact: Tiny hair cells inside the cochlea can be damaged by very loud noise.
fact: In people, damaged hair cells do not grow back, so ear defenders help protect your hearing.
exercise: «The cochlea is a small, spiral tube in the inner ear.» Which part of the ear is the cochlea in? | Middle ear, with the eardrum | Outer ear | *Inner ear
exercise: The cochlea is shaped like a snail shell. What shape is that? | *A spiral | A flat square | A straight line
exercise: The cochlea turns sound vibrations into what? | Heat for the skin | Light signals for the eyes | *Nerve signals that travel to the brain
exercise: «Very loud sounds can damage the hair cells in the cochlea.» Which words in this sentence tell you that loud sounds are harmful? | *Damage the hair cells | Very loud sounds | In the cochlea | Can be heard
test: Which tiny parts inside the cochlea turn sound into nerve signals? | Snail shells | Eardrums | Nerves in the arm | *Hair cells
test: Why are ear defenders useful, according to the context? | They turn sound into nerve signals | They make the cochlea bigger | *They help protect hair cells from very loud noise | They stop you smelling smoke
exam: Which statement is true? | The cochlea makes sounds louder for the eardrum | *The cochlea is a spiral tube in the inner ear that helps us hear | The cochlea is in the outer ear and is shaped like a square | The cochlea is a bone in the middle ear
